============= CLI >>browse --help<< =============
usage: ncrystal_browse [-h] [-s WORD] [-f NAME] [-c] [--names] [-x DATANAME]
                       [--plugins]
                       [PATTERN ...]

Browse and search the data files available to NCrystal (e.g. files in the
standard data library, in the current directory, or in-memory files),
and the loaded plugins.

By default a list of available files is printed, grouped by the source
delivering them, and with a short description extracted from the header
comments of any NCMAT files. The list can be narrowed down by providing
one or more PATTERNs (case-insensitive substrings of file names, or glob
patterns like "Al*.ncmat"), by requiring certain words to be present in
the file names or NCMAT header comments (--search), or by only showing
files from a given source (--factory).

Files marked "(hidden)" are shadowed by files with the same name from a
higher priority source, and are listed with their full name (like
"stdlib::Al_sg225.ncmat"), which can be used to select them explicitly.

positional arguments:
  PATTERN               Only show files whose names match the pattern.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  -s, --search WORD     Only show files which contain WORD in their name or
                        NCMAT header comments (case-insensitive). Can be
                        specified multiple times, in which case all words must
                        be present. Matching comment lines are shown.
  -f, --factory NAME    Only show files delivered by the named factory (e.g.
                        "stdlib" or "virtual").
  -c, --comments        Show full NCMAT header comments of the files.
  --names               Only print the names of the files, one per line
                        (useful for scripting).
  -x, --extract DATANAME
                        Print the full content of DATANAME (e.g. a file name),
                        using the same lookup mechanism as for data in
                        NCrystal cfg-strings. This can therefore also be used
                        to inspect in-memory (or on-demand created) data.
  --plugins             List the currently loaded plugins.

examples:
  ncrystal_browse                     # list all files
  ncrystal_browse Al                  # files with "al" in the name
  ncrystal_browse "*_sg225*"          # files matching a glob pattern
  ncrystal_browse -s vdos -s togo     # search names and header comments
  ncrystal_browse -c Al_sg225.ncmat   # show header comments of a file
  ncrystal_browse -x Al_sg225.ncmat   # show full content of a file
  ncrystal_browse --plugins           # list loaded plugins

